  "description": "What's that bird? Ask MerlinΓÇöthe worldΓÇÖs leading app for birds. Just like magic, Merlin Bird ID will help you solve the mystery.\n\nMerlin helps you identify birds you see and hear. Merlin is unlike any other bird appΓÇöit's powered by eBird, the worldΓÇÖs largest database of bird sightings, sounds, and photos.\n\nMerlin offers four fun ways to identify birds. Answer a few simple questions, upload a photo, record a singing bird, or explore birds in a region.\n\nWhether youΓÇÖre curious about a bird youΓÇÖve seen once or youΓÇÖre hoping to identify every bird you can find, the answers are waiting for you with this free app from the renowned Cornell Lab of Ornithology.\n\nWHY YOUΓÇÖLL LOVE MERLIN\nΓÇó Expert ID tips, range maps, photos, and sounds help you learn about the birds you spot and build birding skills.\nΓÇó Discover a new bird species each day with your own personalized Bird of the Day\nΓÇó Get customized lists of birds you can find where you live or travel - anywhere in the world!\nΓÇó Keep track of your sightingsΓÇöbuild your personal list of the birds you find\n\nMACHINE LEARNING MAGIC\nΓÇó Powered by Visipedia, Merlin Sound ID and Photo ID uses machine learning to identify birds in photos and sounds. Merlin learns to recognize bird species based on training sets of millions of photos and sounds collected by birders at eBird.org, archived in the Macaulay Library at the Cornell Lab of Ornithology.\nΓÇó Merlin delivers the most accurate results thanks to experienced birders, who curate and annotate sightings, photos, and sounds, who are the true magic behind Merlin.\n\nAMAZING CONTENT\nΓÇó Merlin has bird photos, songs, and calls, and identification help for anywhere in the world, including Mexico, Costa Rica, South America, Europe, Africa, the Middle East, India, Australia, Korea, Japan, China, and more.\n\nThe Cornell Lab of OrnithologyΓÇÖs mission is to interpret and conserve the EarthΓÇÖs biological diversity through research, education, and citizen science focused on birds and nature. We are able to offer Merlin for free thanks to the generosity of Cornell Lab members, supporters, and citizen-science contributors.",
